<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/11th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Ascalon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12 August <a href="/years/1099">1099</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · <a href="/today-in-history/august-12">On this day: 12 August</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a> was fought on 12 August 1099 near Ascalon on the coast of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, soon after the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon">Godfrey of Bouillon</a> and the other crusader leaders surprised and routed a Fatimid army led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-afdal">al-Afdal</a>. The victory secured the new crusader conquests, although Ascalon itself remained Fatimid until 1153.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-ascalon.jpg" alt="Battle of Ascalon" width="320" height="181" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Victory Schnetz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1099_Ascalon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1097 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> sought an agreement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and in 1098 they captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> from the Seljuks. The Crusaders had negotiated with the Fatimids on the march, but no compromise was reached: the Fatimids would give up Syria but not <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, which the Crusaders, whose goal was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Church of the Holy Sepulchre</a>, could not accept. Jerusalem fell to the Crusaders on 15 July 1099, and they at once learned that a Fatimid army was coming.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon">Godfrey of Bouillon</a> was named Defender of the Holy Sepulchre on 22 July, and Arnulf of Chocques became patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> on 1 August. On 10 August Godfrey led the army out of Jerusalem towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a>, carrying the relic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a>. Raymond IV of Toulouse and Robert of Normandy stayed behind at first and marched out the next day, once the Egyptian presence was confirmed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Fatimid army was led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-afdal-shahanshah">al-Afdal Shahanshah</a> and numbered perhaps as many as 20,000. It included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> and Ethiopians. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> numbers are unknown; Raymond of Aguilers gave 1,200 knights and 9,000 infantry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-afdal">Al-Afdal</a> camped on the plain of al-Majdal outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a>, apparently unaware that the Crusaders were coming to meet him.</p><p>At dawn on 12 August the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> attacked the camp. According to most accounts, Crusader and Muslim, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> were caught unprepared and the battle was short, although Albert of Aix says it went on for some time. The Fatimids fled to the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a>, were chased into the sea or were killed; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> fled by ship to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and left his camp and treasures to the Crusaders. The Fatimids lost 10,000 infantry and 2,700 residents of Ascalon; Crusader losses are unknown.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The first Muslim attempt to retake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> ended in complete defeat. Godfrey and Raymond both claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a>, and when its garrison learned of the dispute it refused to surrender. Almost all the remaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> then went home, their pilgrimage vows fulfilled, leaving perhaps only a few hundred knights in Jerusalem by the end of the year. Ascalon stayed in Fatimid hands and became a base for yearly attacks on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> until the Crusaders took it in 1153.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Ascalon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In August 1099 he defeated a Fatimid relief army at the <b>Battle of Ascalon</b>, securing the conquest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
